                <pb facs="0297" n="197" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg197"/>

                <head>No. 127.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Everett.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-04-21"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 21,
                        1884</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 231.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to Mr. Sargent’s dispatch
                    No. 264, of the 29th ultimo, concerning the case of Oscar Stern, I have to
                    inform you that the Department has communicated the substance of Mr. Sargent’s
                    dispatch to Mr. Hamburger, of New York, who presented the matter as Mr. Stern’s
                    attorney.</p>
                <p>The Department at the same time advised Mr. Hamburger, that his client, if warned
                    by the German authorities to leave at the expiration of two years, ought not to
                    take the risk of remaining, unless he can obtain permission to do so from the
                    German Government, through your legation.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
